      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right that standard textbooks often fail to adequately describe conditions in darker skin tones, and your focus on the Fitzpatrick scale is crucial for accurate diagnosis. In darker skin types, erythema is often masked by hyperpigmentation, making it difficult to distinguish inflammation from normal skin tone, while basal cell carcinoma frequently presents as a dark brown or black patch rather than the classic pearly papule seen in lighter skin. You must be vigilant for these atypical presentations to avoid missing diagnoses, as the lack of visible redness can lead to delayed treatment in patients with higher melanin levels.",
      "source": "target",
